### 4.1 GLUE

The General Language Understanding Evaluation (GLUE) benchmark Wang et al. ([2018a](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1810.04805v2#bib.bib48)) is a collection of diverse natural language understanding tasks. Detailed descriptions of GLUE datasets are included in Appendix [B.1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1810.04805v2#A2.SS1 "B.1 Detailed Descriptions for the GLUE Benchmark Experiments. ‣ Appendix B Detailed Experimental Setup ‣ BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding").

To fine-tune on GLUE, we represent the input sequence (for single sentence or sentence pairs) as described in Section [3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1810.04805v2#S3 "3 BERT ‣ BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding"), and use the final hidden vector C∈ℝH𝐶superscriptℝ𝐻C\in\mathbb{R}^{H} corresponding to the first input token ([CLS]) as the aggregate representation. The only new parameters introduced during fine-tuning are classification layer weights W∈ℝK×H𝑊superscriptℝ𝐾𝐻W\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times H}, where K𝐾K is the number of labels. We compute a standard classification loss with C𝐶C and W𝑊W, i.e., log⁡(softmax​(C​WT))softmax𝐶superscript𝑊𝑇\log({\rm softmax}(CW^{T})).

